+---
+target: M1-680
+date: 2026-07-23
+base: c5aef23cb1ec86eeb76e57705be189d777db5336
+head: working tree (uncommitted branch m1/M1-680-sanitizer-flag-position)
+verdict: FINDINGS
+findings_count:
+  critical: 0
+  high: 1
+  medium: 0
+  low: 0
+out_of_model_count: 0
+disposition: |
+  The high finding was independently verified in the driver session by
+  reading the code paths it cites: InboundRouter.handleSlash passes the
+  whole, possibly multi-line, normalized body to the handler (no per-line
+  command dispatch; normalize's split("\n") rejoins the lines), and
+  ListSourcesArgs.parse tokenizes with split("\\s+") — Java \s includes
+  \n — so a flag on any line after the command word dispatches all=true
+  while the line-scoped tokenizer refused to match across \n. The
+  regression half was confirmed against main: the pre-diff pattern
+  joined words with \s+, which spans \n, so adjacent
+  /list-sources\n--all was redacted before this branch and was not on
+  it. Remediation: drop the line bound — the argument run spans the
+  whole message (the tokenizer's separator set is now exactly the ASCII
+  \s set and the scan is one monotonic pass over the input); ticket
+  acceptance items, Notes, and docs/spec/security.md were rewritten to
+  the message-scoped rule. A fourth audit of the remediated diff is
+  required before review.
+  (File note: the original r3 verdict lived in target/ and was wiped by
+  mvn clean; this record preserves its text verbatim. The r2 verdict
+  file was lost the same way — its findings are summarized in the
+  ticket frontmatter and in .scratch/M1-680-handoff.md.)
+---
+RED-TEAM VERDICT: FINDINGS
+
+FINDINGS:
+  - CATEGORY: AUDIT-EVASION
+    SEVERITY: high
+    PROMISE: docs/spec/security.md §LLM output sanitizer, "Flag position
+             mirrors the parser's own scan" (added by this diff): "A
+             flag-bearing closed-list entry matches its flag at **any
+             position on the command's own line** ... The scan is bounded
+             to a **single line**, because the dispatcher treats one line
+             as one command ... Whitespace is read the way the parser's
+             `split("\s+")` reads it — `\r` is an intra-line token
+             separator (the router splits lines on `\n` alone) ...
+             mirroring the dispatch." Plus the general commitment: "the
+             sanitizer closes the social-engineering surface where a
+             small LLM emits plausible-looking admin commands across any
+             of the surfaces above. Every match is audit-logged
+             (per-occurrence, not throttled)." And §Source URL visibility:
+             the `/list-sources --all` listing discloses "every URL added
+             via /add-source" — the entry exists to keep that catalogue
+             out of LLM output.
+    GAP: The premise "the dispatcher treats one line as one command" /
+         "the router splits lines on `\n` alone" is false for argument
+         parsing. InboundRouter.handleSlash (InboundRouter.java:1410-1420)
+         resolves the command NAME from the first token
+         (`normalized.split("\\s+", 2)[0]`) but passes the ENTIRE,
+         possibly multi-line, `normalized` body to `handler.handle`.
+         `normalize` (InboundRouter.java:1625-1661) preserves internal
+         `\n` (it only trims leading/trailing whitespace). The handler's
+         parser `ListSourcesArgs.parse`
+         (ListSourcesCommandHandler.java:474-495) does
+         `rawText.trim().split("\\s+")` — and Java `\s` includes `\n` —
+         so it scans EVERY whitespace-delimited token across ALL lines
+         and sets `all=true` for a `--all` on any line, not just the
+         command word's line. The sanitizer's tokenizer does the
+         opposite: `redactFlagEntry` (LlmOutputSanitizer.java:293-341)
+         hard-splits the input on `\n` (line 304, `input.indexOf('\n', …)`
+         drives a per-line outer loop) and `isHorizontalWhitespace`
+         (LlmOutputSanitizer.java:400-402) deliberately EXCLUDES `\n`, so
+         it refuses to match a flag on any line after the command word.
+         A flag placed on a following line therefore dispatches
+         `all=true` at the parser while evading the sanitizer — no
+         `[redacted command]`, no WARN, no `LLM_OUTPUT_SANITIZED` audit
+         row (the same silent-non-match property the round-1 high finding
+         flagged, reintroduced on the newline axis). This is also a
+         REGRESSION: the pre-diff regex joined words with `\s+`, which
+         spans `\n`, so `/list-sources\n--all` (adjacent across a newline)
+         WAS matched before this diff and is not now.
+    REPRO: A prompt-injected reply, or a hostile/compromised LLM endpoint
+           (§Trust boundaries item 9 puts the endpoint's reply in scope
+           and lets it choose every byte), returns two lines:
+           `/list-sources` then, on the next line, `--all` (equivalently
+           `/list-sources --page 1\n--all`, or with a blank line between —
+           `split("\s+")` collapses the run). The sanitizer ships it
+           verbatim. A bot admin reading the reply selects the block
+           starting at `/list-sources` and pastes it; the router sees a
+           body starting with `/`, resolves `list-sources`, and
+           `ListSourcesArgs.parse` splits on the `\n`, finds `--all`, and
+           sets `all=true`. The handler returns the deployment-wide source
+           catalogue (§Source URL visibility) — every source URL across
+           the deployment — with no `LLM_OUTPUT_SANITIZED` audit row ever
+           written for the leak, exactly the outcome the closed-list entry
+           and its per-occurrence audit commitment exist to prevent.
+    SUGGESTED-FIX-CLASS: input-sanitization
+
+OUT-OF-MODEL:
+  - (none)
